{
  "teamId": 115,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Colorado Rockies arrived with genuine expectations, and their April validated the optimism almost immediately. Nolan Arenado launched the tone of the year with a first-week home run, and Charlie Blackmon followed weeks later with a go-ahead shot that signaled this offense was ready to carry a team. At 16-10 through April, Colorado had staked its claim as a legitimate contender in the National League West, powered by a lineup that could punish any pitcher on any given night.\n\nMay and June saw the Rockies sustain the momentum even as the bullpen began flashing warning signs. The offense rose to meet every challenge — Ian Desmond's three-run bomb in mid-June, Arenado's stunning three-run walk-off swing on June 18th to reverse a Jake McGee disaster, and a clutch triple just two days later that kept the train rolling. At 15-12 in June, Colorado was still winning despite the cracks forming in the relief corps. The team's heart was obvious: whenever the bullpen faltered, the lineup would claw back.\n\nAugust told the story of Greg Holland's unraveling. The closer who had been counted on as a stabilizing force became the season's most painful subplot, surrendering crushing home runs in a stretch that threatened to sink the Wild Card race. Yet the offense refused to surrender — Pat Valaika, Charlie Blackmon, and the indomitable Arenado kept answering back. Colorado went 12-15 in August, their only losing month, but recovered smartly in September at 15-13, riding Arenado's 33rd home run deep into a pennant chase that ultimately delivered an 87-win season and a Wild Card berth.\n\nThe October stage brought Chase Field and the Arizona Diamondbacks, and it brought heartbreak. Jon Gray gave up early runs including a Paul Goldschmidt three-run home run that put the Rockies in a hole they could never fully escape. The bullpen — Holland again, and Pat Neshek — allowed critical runs in the late innings. Trevor Story hit a solo shot to keep hope flickering, but A.J. Pollock's RBI triple off Holland in the eighth inning sealed Colorado's fate. A season of genuine triumph, 87 wins and a playoff appearance for a franchise that had known too many losing summers, ended in a single cruel October night.",
